## Executive summary

1. The loudest instrument in an MWAA hold room during a slot-constrained departure is not the airport public-address system. It is a gate agent's handheld, layered over concessionaire audio and tenant televisions. MWAA specifies the shell; the airline, tenant, and handler sources on top sit outside the base-building spec.[^1]

2. A standard written only into the base-terminal design volume will govern only the shell. The lever is the tenant design volumes, airline lease exhibits, and commissioning protocol — the instruments through which MWAA already regulates concession fitouts, gate finishes, and substantial-completion acceptance.[^2]

3. Code governs emergency communication, not the boarding call. NFPA 72 requires a Speech Transmission Index of at least 0.45 at 90 percent of measurement locations and an average of 0.50 for the emergency communication system;[^3] code permits but does not mandate STIPA-instrumented verification. ACRP Research Report 175 lifts the working target to STI 0.50 with at least 10 dB(A) of signal-to-noise margin in pier hold rooms.[^4] Elevating that ACRP guideline to owner-required performance, and mandating STIPA in occupied conditions at commissioning, is precisely what only a design-manual amendment can do.

4. Redundant channels are federal law — for whichever actor owns each surface. 14 CFR Part 382 binds carriers on carrier-controlled facilities and televisions.[^5] Section 504 and 49 CFR Part 27 bind MWAA on airport-controlled space.[^6] 28 CFR §35.160 requires "primary consideration" of the passenger's requested aid.[^7] The concessionaire's television inside a leased restaurant sits in a coverage gap that only the tenant handbook closes. DOT is actively rulemaking to tighten Part 382 toward real-time accessible information; a standard that reads as reducing information flow will be judged against that direction.[^8] Chennai 2017 is the operational-failure benchmark.[^9]

5. DCA needs a higher announcement floor than IAD, not a lower one. DCA is capped at 67 hourly slots on a single main runway; the exposure of a missed boarding is asymmetric there because slots are scarce and unrecoverable at the schedule level, not because any specific event is priced.[^10] Under §9.05.8 of the 2025 Use and Lease, MWAA receives $1 million per new beyond-perimeter DCA pair conditioned on a $2 DCA-vs-IAD CPE advantage; the current gap is $2.36, leaving $0.36 of headroom.[^11] At the sizing this document contemplates, §9.05.8 is a monitoring constraint, not a live threat.

6. The timing is binding for Concourses C and D. Concourse E opens in September 2026 — its ceilings are poured; it is commissioning-baseline material, not a design-decision opportunity.[^12] The July 2026 joint MWAA–United–USDOT announcement puts more than 5 million square feet of IAD terminal space into design at an order-of-magnitude of $20 billion to $22.5 billion; the C/D program brief is the next decision point.[^13] Acoustic ceiling treatment costs $5 to $15 per square foot in new construction and $18 to $45 per square foot as retrofit.[^14] Every C/D square foot that ships without acoustic, PA-zoning, and information specifications is a square foot the Authority will pay two to four times as much to fix.

7. The affordability envelope is spending headroom. MWAA's 2025A financing has IAD signatory CPE rising from $12.77 today to $20.71 by 2030 under the signed $9.0 billion Capital Construction Program.[^15] The entire MWAA-wide quiet-by-design capital stack sizes at $12 million to $40 million — 0.13 to 0.44 percent of the signed CCP, and $0.03 to $0.11 per enplanement across combined IAD+DCA traffic.[^16]

8. Adopt the standard as an amendment to the Airports Authority Design Manual, name it the Terminal Acoustic and Information Performance Standard, and route it as a Design Manual amendment rather than a discrete capital program. Never use the word "quiet" in the resolution.

