## The hold room MWAA does not own

Start at Gate 35 of DCA Terminal 2 on a weekday morning. The 1997 Pelli National Hall opens into a long, hard-surfaced concourse. American Airlines, which holds roughly 53 percent of DCA departures, is turning slot-capped narrowbodies against a 67-slot-per-hour cap.[^17] A gate agent picks up a handheld and calls Group 4 in a voice that competes with a muted-but-audible television at the concession across the way, a rolling wheel of announcements from a neighboring gate whose zone the agent cannot silence, and the HVAC that runs the length of the pier. A passenger with hearing aids two rows back leans in. A passenger who does not carry a smartphone watches for the visual cue that is not there. The boarding pass in her hand says Group 3.

MWAA owns the room. It does not own the sound.

That sentence is the operative fact of a quiet-by-design standard at MWAA. The Authority controls the shell — ceiling, finishes, base-building PA — and specifies it through Volumes 1 and 2 of the Airports Authority Design Manual. It controls tenant fitouts, gate hold-room standards, and concession finish palettes through Volumes 2 and 3.[^2] It controls emergency notification through NFPA 72 compliance in the fire-alarm envelope. It does not control the airline gate microphone; gate-PA operation inside the leased envelope is airline-run in practice, and whether the executed 2025 Use and Lease explicitly names broadcast rights is a diligence item for MWAA General Counsel.[^1] It does not control the tenant television, which lives inside the concessionaire's lease. It does not control the ground handler's handheld, which lives inside another contract entirely.

That is not a reason to abandon the standard. It is the reason to write it where the microphones actually live.

## What the peers actually did

Every airport the trade press calls "silent" governs exceptions. London City Airport's silent-terminal policy, in effect since 2008, expressly retains announcements for short-notice gate changes, disruption, and FIDS failure.[^18] Helsinki-Vantaa, silent since 1 June 2015, retains gate and emergency announcements; trade coverage a few years in reported that Finavia had added banners under flight-information displays telling passengers to track time themselves.[^19] Amsterdam Schiphol describes itself as running "virtually no announcements" and pairs the policy with a formal accessibility program for deaf and blind passengers.[^20] Copenhagen zones quiet by gate area rather than airport-wide.[^21] San Francisco — the only documented U.S. large-hub program — reports a roughly 40 percent reduction in boarding-area announcements and about 90 minutes per day removed from the International Terminal, alongside a public record of passenger complaints about missed information.[^22] Chennai's 2017 rollout produced a disability-community backlash serious enough that airport officials publicly conceded "no separate provisions for the visually-challenged are available."[^9]

Two conclusions follow. No peer operator has published measured missed-boarding, missed-connection, or IROPS data attributable to a silent-airport policy. Any argument built on peer branding is design conservatism, not measured outcome, and should be defended as such. And every one of these programs is an exception-governance regime, not the absence of a PA. The interesting variable at MWAA is not how much silence to inherit; it is what the exceptions are, who authorizes them, and in which zone.

## The floors the standard inherits — and the one it adds

Before MWAA writes anything, it inherits three regulatory floors. NFPA 72 requires an STI of at least 0.45 at 90 percent of measurement locations within each Acoustically Distinguishable Space, and an average of 0.50, for the emergency communication system.[^3] It permits but does not mandate STIPA-instrumented verification; a listening test satisfies the code absent another code or owner requirement. That single fact is the affirmative case for a design-manual amendment. Elevating ACRP Research Report 175 targets — STI 0.50 for terminal PA, at least 10 dB(A) of signal-to-noise margin in pier hold rooms — from guideline to owner-required performance, and mandating STIPA measurement in occupied conditions at commissioning, are things only the owner specification can do.[^4] The authority having jurisdiction — MWAA's own code function under its federal lease, or a county fire marshal; the specific AHJ at each airport is itself a diligence item — enforces the code floor at certificate of occupancy. The owner specification enforces the delta above it.

The second inherited floor is accessibility, and it runs through three different regulations for three different actors. 14 CFR Part 382 binds air carriers on facilities they own, lease, or control, including carrier-controlled televisions capable of captioning.[^5] Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act and 49 CFR Part 27 bind MWAA as a federal-funding recipient on airport-controlled communication.[^6] 28 CFR §35.160 binds MWAA as a Title II entity to "primary consideration" of a passenger's requested aid.[^7] The concessionaire's television inside a leased restaurant sits in a coverage gap that no federal regulation squarely occupies — which is precisely why the tenant handbook is the lever, and why DOT's active Part 382 rulemaking toward real-time accessible information matters even for surfaces Part 382 does not directly touch.[^8] A standard that reads as reducing information flow will be judged against a tightening federal baseline.

## Two airports, two calibrations

DCA and IAD are not the same operation, and the standard should not pretend they are. DCA closed 2025 at 24.9 million passengers, down 5.3 percent year over year and its lowest total since 2022, absorbing the January 2025 midair collision, a seven-week fall federal shutdown, and softer federal-workforce travel demand.[^23] It runs on a single main runway, capped at 67 hourly slots, with a heavy federal-official and business mix and a short curb-to-gate journey. Section 502 of the 2024 FAA Reauthorization added ten beyond-perimeter exemptions, awarded in December 2024 with service required to begin by 17 March 2025.[^10]

Do not price the DCA argument in denied-boarding dollars. Denied boardings are an oversale event; a passenger who misses a boarding call is a no-show, an event GAO puts at under 1 percent of boardings and one whose cost is mostly borne by the passenger. A U.S. narrowbody delay minute runs about $98 on the industry system average — a useful order of magnitude, not a DCA-specific figure.[^24] The DCA argument is exposure, not event cost: slot scarcity makes a schedule-level miss asymmetric in a way it is not at IAD, and §9.05.8 monetizes that exposure indirectly by tying $1 million per beyond-perimeter pair to a CPE-gap covenant. The current gap of $2.36 gives the Authority room to maneuver; the standard's job is to keep DCA's audible channel reliable enough that the standard itself is not the thing that starts consuming the $0.36 of headroom.[^11]

IAD ran roughly 29 million passengers in 2025, including 10.5 million international — both all-time highs.[^25] Curb-to-gate journeys are longer, passenger populations more multilingual, and transfer flows more dependent on visual and digital redundancy than on English audio. The standard's calibration at IAD is density and redundancy of the visual and digital channels; the calibration at DCA is intelligibility and reliability of the audible one.

One standard. Two calibrations. Written as one common intelligibility floor with an explicit DCA variance for hold-room announcement density and IROPS PA envelope.

## The counter-case, honestly

The strongest argument against writing a new standard is that MWAA does not need one. NFPA 72, ADA Title II, ACAA, and a competent commissioning specification already require what a quiet-by-design standard would require; ACRP 175 already exists as a project-level reference; a paragraph in each consultant's scope naming ACRP 175 and requiring STIPA verification in occupied conditions would deliver most of the value without a board resolution. The Board has not asked for this; the White House and USDOT have made the Dulles program politically visible; and the January 2025 DCA midair is still in memory. A "quiet" framing at MWAA in 2026 will be read against that memory.

The concession the standard must therefore make is threefold. It should not be sold as a new requirement; it should be sold as the integration layer across requirements that today live in different consultants' scopes on different projects with no owner for the seam. It should not carry the word "quiet" in any external MWAA document; it should carry the name Terminal Acoustic and Information Performance Standard, for exactly the reason Schiphol and Helsinki brand silence externally but govern intelligibility internally. And it should say nothing about airline announcement scripting inside the leased envelope — a fight MWAA does not need to open before the next U&L review.

What the standard should say is what the base building must achieve, what the tenant volumes must achieve on top of it, what the airline lease exhibits require by way of gate-PA discipline for slot-constrained departures, and what commissioning must verify in occupied conditions before substantial completion. That is the integration layer. Berlin Brandenburg is the cautionary tale for what happens when it is missing: a life-safety and systems-integration failure against a frozen architectural design — the class of failure that includes the PA/ECS envelope — with an initial €2 billion budget that closed at €7.3 billion and a nine-year schedule slip.[^26] Denver's automated baggage system is the same class of failure in a different subsystem: contract escalation from $193 million to over $400 million, a 16-month opening delay at roughly $1 million per day of carrying cost, and abandonment in August 2005.[^27] Both were technology aspirations colliding with frozen envelopes. Both were preventable at design.

One more piece of the argument helps the design-manual route: majority-in-interest carve-outs in U.S. use-and-lease agreements commonly exempt safety- and security-mandated capital, which suggests the amendment may sit outside airline veto if it is written as a safety/accessibility performance standard rather than a discretionary premium.[^28] Whether the executed MWAA 2025 U&L follows that pattern is a diligence question — the same one already flagged for the broadcast-rights point.

## Where it gets written, and by when

Adopt the standard as an amendment to the Airports Authority Design Manual, scoped inside the FY26 revision cycle, in three tiers. The mandatory floor applies to both airports: NFPA 72 STI verified under IEC 60268-16 STIPA in occupied conditions; ACRP 175 signal-to-noise margin in every pier hold room; a redundant-channel obligation naming audible, visual/digital, and staffed delivery for every safety-critical, boarding-critical, and IROPS message; PA-push authority anchored to a named position with pre-authorized override; and an emergency-notification stack specified on generator plus UPS plus local battery, with a duty-officer analog fallback for the day the managed-IT layer goes down as it did on 19 July 2024.[^29] Recommended practice covers NC/RC targets by space type, quiet-zone locations, and employee sound-exposure monitoring under OSHA 1910.95.[^30] Context-dependent options cover the DCA variance clause and preservation carve-outs for the Saarinen Main Terminal at IAD and the 1941 Terminal 1 at DCA.

Route it as a Design Manual amendment inside existing consultant fees. General counsel should confirm before the Board memo is drafted whether the amendment triggers majority-in-interest thresholds in the 2025 Use and Lease and whether the safety/accessibility carve-out applies. If the answer is board resolution, the executive owner is the President and CEO with the Chief Operating Officer and Vice President of Design and Construction as co-sponsors; the first 90-day action is instructing the Design Manual working group to insert acoustic and information performance criteria into Volume 1, the IAD tenant volumes, and the DCA parallel volumes before the Concourse C and D program brief is issued. Concourse E, opening in September 2026, becomes the commissioning-data baseline against which C and D will be judged.[^12] The leading indicator is that STIPA data at commissioning and again 60 days after opening. The failure mode to guard against is scope migration from an acoustic-and-information envelope into an announcement-policy standard the airlines contest under the Use and Lease. Stop if the MII review indicates the standard becomes an incremental capital line item outside the safety carve-out; in that case renegotiate as a side-letter to the next U&L review, not adopted as an aspirational document.

## A last image

Return to the hold room at DCA Terminal 2. Under the standard, the ceiling is different, the wall absorption is different, and the base-building loudspeakers are laid out to reach every seat with an STI the AHJ will accept in occupied conditions. The airline gate PA is unchanged; American still calls Group 3. The concessionaire's television is captioned and muted, per the amended tenant handbook. The FIDS updates within an SLA that survives a gate change inside T-25 minutes. A passenger with hearing aids two rows back hears the boarding call cleanly. A passenger without a smartphone reads the same information off a display. The gate agent, no longer competing with adjacent-zone spillover, spends the next four minutes with the family that missed the boarding sequence rather than with the microphone.

Nothing in that room is quiet in the marketing sense. Every message that matters lands. That is the standard MWAA can write, and it is the only one worth writing before the concrete cures.

